Let me walk through the three forms calmly, the way I would explain them to a junior joining our fixed-deposit team.

The interface-based projection is the simplest to write. You declare an interface whose getter names match the entity property names, and Spring Data generates a proxy class that is populated with only those columns. For example `fdRepo.findByAccountId("SAV2001")` returning `List<FdSummary>` where `FdSummary` only has `getFdId()` and `getMaturityAmount()` generates `SELECT f.fd_id, f.maturity_amount FROM fixed_deposits f WHERE f.account_id = ?`. The important nuance is that there are two sub-flavours. A CLOSED projection declares getters that map directly to properties, and Spring Data can optimise the SQL to fetch only those columns. An OPEN projection uses `@Value("#{target.principal + target.maturityAmount}")` with a SpEL expression; because SpEL can reference any part of the entity, Spring Data must fetch the WHOLE entity first and then compute, so you lose the column-narrowing benefit.

```java
// CLOSED interface projection — only two columns fetched
public interface FdSummary {
    String getFdId();
    java.math.BigDecimal getMaturityAmount();
}
List<FdSummary> findByAccountId(String accountId);
// SQL: SELECT f.fd_id, f.maturity_amount FROM fixed_deposits f WHERE f.account_id = ?
```

The class/DTO projection with a JPQL constructor expression is the most explicit and usually the fastest. You call `new` directly inside the JPQL and Spring Data invokes that constructor per row. For example `@Query("SELECT new com.bank.fd.dto.FdDto(f.fdId, f.maturityAmount) FROM FixedDeposit f WHERE f.status = :status")` produces plain `FdDto` objects that never enter the persistence context, so there is no proxy overhead, no dirty-check snapshot, and no lazy-proxy risk. A key detail: the constructor parameter order must match the SELECT clause exactly, and you must use the fully qualified class name in classic JPQL. Since Spring Data 3 you can also simply declare a `record` as the method return type and Spring Data maps column-by-column automatically, provided the record component names line up with the selected aliases.

```java
public record FdDto(String fdId, java.math.BigDecimal maturityAmount) {}

@Query("SELECT new com.bank.fd.dto.FdDto(f.fdId, f.maturityAmount) " +
       "FROM FixedDeposit f WHERE f.status = :status")
List<FdDto> findDtoByStatus(@Param("status") String status);
```

The dynamic projection is the most flexible. You declare the finder with a generic type parameter and a `Class<T>` argument. At runtime the caller decides which projection to materialise. For example `fdRepo.findByAccountId("SAV2001", FdSummary.class)` returns lightweight interface proxies, `fdRepo.findByAccountId("SAV2001", FdDto.class)` returns DTO records, and `fdRepo.findByAccountId("SAV2001", FixedDeposit.class)` returns full managed entities — all from ONE method signature. This is extremely useful for a search endpoint that serves both a compact list view and a detailed drill-down from the same query.

```java
<T> List<T> findByAccountId(String accountId, Class<T> type);
// caller picks:
List<FdSummary> light = fdRepo.findByAccountId("SAV2001", FdSummary.class);
List<FixedDeposit> full = fdRepo.findByAccountId("SAV2001", FixedDeposit.class);
```

On performance, the DTO/class projection is the senior-level default for read endpoints because it fetches the fewest columns and skips persistence-context management entirely. The closed interface projection is almost as good but adds a small proxy layer. Full entities are slowest because every row enters the persistence context with a snapshot for dirty checking. A real caution I always give: nested or association-based interface projections can silently fire an extra query per row, so I keep projections flat and reach for a DTO when I need computed or joined values.

